# Institutional Grammar IG 2.0

**Type:** Method **Status:** Active **Last Updated:** 2026-06-12

---

## Core Synthesis

Institutional Grammar 2.0 (IG 2.0) is the rule-level coding methodology applied across three papers (P2, P3, fsQCA chapter) and is the method that generates the empirical evidence base for all three PRIS signature constructs. The ADICO syntax — Attributes, Deontic, aIm, Conditions, Or-else — parses governance instruments at the institutional statement level, enabling systematic, replicable extraction of rule structure from regulatory text. This note records what IG 2.0 is, how it has been applied in the PRIS corpus, where the authoritative coding lives, and the methodological decisions made. It is not a tutorial. The authoritative source is Siddiki et al. (2019); the PRIS codebook is in NVivo; the coded output is in `V12_SOM.docx` (49-instrument corpus) and will be generated for the 10-case fsQCA sample in Q2–Q3 2026.

## Method Profile

- **Type:** Rule-level text coding; institutional analysis
- **Syntax:** ADICO — Attributes (A), Deontic (D), aIm (I), Conditions (C), Or-else (O)
- **Ontology:** Statements are classified as Rules (ADICO complete), Norms (ADIC without O), Shared Strategies (AIC without D or O)
- **Software:** NVivo (coding environment); inter-coder reliability via Cohen's κ
- **Key anchors in PRIS:** Siddiki et al. (2019) — IG 2.0 specification; Crawford & Ostrom (1995) — original IG grammar; Basurto et al. (2010) — application protocol; Frantz & Siddiki (2021) — IG 2.0 refinements

## Application in PRIS Corpus

### P2 — DPI Governance (JSIS)

- **Corpus:** 12 governance documents; 248 coded institutional statements across 4 DPI cases (PM-KISAN, Aadhaar Face Authentication, Account Aggregator, PM-JAY)
- **Key finding:** Of 11 Rules, 0 govern citizen-facing fallback procedures — system-facing governance is ADICO-complete; citizen-facing provisions are ADIC or AI only
- **Status:** ✅ Coding complete; inter-coder reliability documented

### P3 — BFSI AI Governance (JEIM)

- **Corpus:** 18 binding instruments (2012–2024); 450 requirement units extracted; 91 AI-relevant units coded
- **Key finding:** Coordination-pair classification (parallelism / convergence / contradiction) derived from ADICO comparison across RBI, SEBI, CERT-In instruments; 0 cross-references between RBI and SEBI units
- **Status:** ✅ Coding complete; 90% inter-coder agreement documented; 2 edge cases revised

### fsQCA Thesis Chapter

- **Corpus:** 10 cases (final list pending TAC); rule-level coding to generate condition calibration scores (EA, SR, RL, SI)
- **Status:** 🔴 0% — begins Q2 2026 after case list locked
- **Target:** Cohen's κ ≥ 0.75 on 20% double-coded subsample

## Methodological Decisions (Applied in PRIS)

|Decision|Choice|Rationale|
|---|---|---|
|Unit of analysis|Institutional statement (not paragraph or clause)|Enables cross-instrument comparability|
|Deontic classification|Must/shall = Rule; Should/ought = Norm; May = Shared Strategy|Standard IG 2.0 protocol; applied consistently across P2, P3, fsQCA|
|Or-else coding|Explicit penalty text OR documented regulatory consequence|Avoids over-coding implicit enforcement|
|Inter-coder protocol|20% random subsample double-coded by second coder; Cohen's κ reported|Standard reliability threshold κ ≥ 0.75|
|AI-relevance filter|P3: applied to 450 requirement units to isolate 91 AI-relevant units|Reduces scope to PRIS-relevant coding; method and filter documented in SOM|

